 ## Subtopics
 
-### Slicemap Boundary Voxelization
-A technique to represent a binary 3D grid of voxels using a single 2D texture, where the $k$-th bit of the RGBA representation of pixel $(i, j)$ encodes the value of voxel $(i, j, k)$ [S2, unofficial]. It renders the scene and, for each fragment, if its depth lies in the $k$-th slice, outputs a color having only the $k$-th bit activated [S2, unofficial]. It uses a bitwise OR blending mode to combine all fragments projecting to the same pixel, allowing tracking of all fragments in a single pass rather than discarding all but the closest [S2, unofficial]. Unlike Conservative Voxelization via Depth Range, it does not compute geometric intersection bounds but relies on depth quantization and bitwise operations [S2, unofficial].
+### [Slicemap Boundary Voxelization](3344-slicemap-boundary-voxelization.md)
+Slicemap Boundary Voxelization is a technique to represent a binary 3D grid of voxels using a single 2D texture, where the $k$-th bit of the RGBA representation of pixel $(i, j)$ encodes the occupancy of voxel $(i, j, k)$. It solves the problem of real-time voxelization by leveraging graphics hardware to test geometry for intersection with an encompassing grid of cells, allowing the tracking of all fragments in a single pass rather than discarding all but the closest. Unlike [Conservative Voxelization via Depth Range](3129-conservative-voxelization-via-depth-range.md), which encodes depth ranges into occupancy bits, slicemaps explicitly map specific depth slices to specific bit positions, enabling efficient accumulation of all intersecting geometry via bitwise operations.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of triangles, as each triangle is processed in a single pass (or few passes for MRT) [S1, unofficial]. The intersection computation is constant time per triangle (12 intersections) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(res_x \times res_y \times \frac{res_z}{32})$ for the output texture [S1, unofficial]. The lookup table is small ($32 \times 32$ or $512 \times 128$ texels) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method leverages GPU native 4D vector processing for intersection calculations, significantly accelerating the depth range computation [S1, unofficial]. MRT support allows processing 128 slices in a single pass, reducing the number of rendering passes required for high-resolution volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Accuracy:** Theoretically accurate; misses no voxels. The number of redundant voxels is no more than 0.1% of the total number [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires hardware support for MRT and bitwise blending operations. The lookup table size grows with the number of slices if not using MRT [S1, unofficial]. Parallel line and degenerate triangle cases require careful handling to maintain correctness [S1, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The method performs constant-time operations per fragment for derivative calculation and intersection checks, optimized by GPU 4D vector processing. Memory usage is dominated by the lookup table texture ($32 \times 32$ or $512 \times 128$ texels) and the frame buffer.
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+Implemented on an AMD AthlonXP 3000+ CPU, 512 MB RAM, and NVidia 6600 GT video card using Cg shaders [S1, unofficial]. The approach achieves conservatively correct results with negligible errors (redundant voxels $\le 0.1\%$ of total) [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance via GPU rasterization; conservative correctness ensures no voxels are missed; efficient bitwise encoding allows processing 128 slices in a single pass with MRT [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited by GPU floating-point precision, requiring error correction constants; alternative pre-computation approaches using lookup tables for intersection codes are faster in theory but expensive in hardware due to switch-case structures [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+Use when real-time voxelization of triangle meshes is required and conservative accuracy is critical. Not suitable when hardware does not support MRT or logical 'OR' blending.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in a paper on conservative voxelization, which proposed computing the depth range of a projected triangle over a fragment rectangle to encode occupancy bits into a texture [S1, unofficial]. It builds upon the concept of slicemaps, where a 3D grid is represented by a 2D texture using bits of RGBA channels [S2, unofficial]. The approach addresses the limitations of pre-computation based approaches that use switch-case structures and costly intersection point location computations, offering a more hardware-friendly implementation using native GPU vector operations [S1, unofficial].
+The method was proposed in a paper titled "Conservative Voxelization" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the need for efficient, hardware-accelerated voxelization techniques that maintain geometric accuracy. The approach leverages standard graphics pipeline features (vertex processing, rasterization, fragment shaders) to compute depth ranges, avoiding the need for explicit geometric intersection algorithms on the CPU or specialized hardware.
 
